  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nAn inverted surface trough will allow for isolated to widely \nscattered chances of showers and storms this afternoon and evening, \nprimarily along and south of I-40. Latest mesoanalysis and model \nderived soundings suggest MLCAPE will be limited to around 1000 \nJ/kg. While lapse rates and shear will be poor, DCAPE near 1000 J/kg \ncould contribute to locally gusty winds under any activity that \nbecomes tall enough to produce lightning.\n\nActivity will wane into the overnight and lead to a couple of dry \ndays as surface high pressure takes control Tuesday and Wednesday. \nThe main thing to note will be a slight warming trend as H5 heights \nof 596-598dam build into the region behind a trough axis departing \nthe eastern seaboard. While not expecting record breaking heat like \nour last heat wave, above normal temperatures persist. \n\nBy Thursday an upper level trough and associated jet is expected to \ndip into the Great Lakes and northern Ohio Valley region. An \naccompanying cold front will provide an axis of forcing leading to a \nreturn of showers and storms by Thursday afternoon into Friday. \nLack of shear locally suggest limited chances of any strong to \nsevere weather. The main concern may be some training \nprecip/isolated flooding due to deep-layer flow being nearly \nparallel to the front. An additional weak shortwave may continue \nscattered to rain chances Saturday, with decreasing chances Sunday\ninto early next week as a ridge builds into the region.",
